### Install pg driver

Source: https://github.com/typeorm/typeorm/blob/master/docs/docs/drivers/postgres.md

Install the PostgreSQL driver for TypeORM using npm.

```shell
npm install pg
```

--------------------------------

### QueryFailedError class with generic driverError and property spread

Source: https://github.com/typeorm/typeorm/blob/master/packages/typeorm/src/error/QueryFailedError.ts

The QueryFailedError class accepts a generic type parameter T for the driverError. In the constructor, all properties of the driverError (except 'name') are spread onto the error instance via ObjectUtils.assign. This means when the PostgreSQL driver throws an error with code '23505', that code becomes directly accessible on the QueryFailedError instance (e.g., (error as QueryFailedError<{code: string}>).code).

```typescript
export class QueryFailedError<T extends Error = Error> extends TypeORMError {
    constructor(
        readonly query: string,
        readonly parameters: any[] | ObjectLiteral | undefined,
        readonly driverError: T,
    ) {
        super(
            driverError
                .toString()
                .replace(/^error: /, "")
                .replace(/^Error: /, "")
                .replace(/^Request/, ""),
        )

        if (driverError) {
            const {
                name: _, // eslint-disable-line
                ...otherProperties
            } = driverError

            ObjectUtils.assign(this, {
                ...otherProperties,
            })
        }
    }
}
```

--------------------------------

### PostgresQueryRunner throwing QueryFailedError with native driver error

Source: https://github.com/typeorm/typeorm/blob/master/packages/typeorm/src/driver/postgres/PostgresQueryRunner.ts

When a Postgres query fails, the raw pg driver error (which includes code: '23505' for unique constraint violations) is passed directly as the driverError parameter to QueryFailedError. The driverError is preserved as a readonly property, and all its properties (including code) are spread onto the error instance.

```typescript
throw new QueryFailedError(query, parameters, err)
```

